WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.470
Star Trek and Star Wars are often looked upon
as two sides of the same sci-fi coin, locked

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.880
in a never-ending battle of philosophy versus
action that will last until the end of time…

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.650
or at least until the arguing nerds need a
nap.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:17.270
In truth, both franchises probably owe their
continued existence to each other.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.960
George Lucas admitted that Star Trek basically
paved the way for Star Wars.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.630
And William Shatner conceded that Paramount
only became interested in giving Star Trek

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:32.000
a real shot on the big screen after Star Wars
took the world by storm in 1977.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.920
Generally, fans pledge outright allegiance
to one franchise over the other, but that's

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.050
an idea that the following actors chose to
ignore entirely.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:46.449
Here are a few actors who visited both a galaxy
far, far away and the Alpha Quadrant.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:48.020
George Takei

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.600
Star Trek legend George Takei has been a part
of the Enterprise from the very beginning,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.789
first appearing as Lieutenant Sulu in the
original series, and going on to reprise the

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:02.230
role for six Star Trek feature films, video
games, and even an episode of Voyager.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.570
But like many Star Trek alumni, it was through
voice work that he made the transition between

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.150
the two biggest universes in sci-fi.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.640
Takei became the most high profile name to
take the leap when he landed the part of General

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.780
Lok Durd in the Clone Wars animated series.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.550
"I congratulate you on your good fortune."

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.750
When asked about appearing in Star Wars, Takei
remarked that he didn't consider it to be

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.300
jumping ship, telling Comic Book Resources,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.060
"The Star Trek philosophy is to embrace the
diversity of life, and Star Wars is a part

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.299
of that diversity.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:37.050
[...] Star Trek is science fiction and Star
Wars is more science fantasy.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.610
But with the episodes of Star Wars: The Clone
Wars that I worked on, I think there is a

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:41.790
merging there."

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.220
Star Trek and Star Wars living together in
harmony?

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.840
"Oh my!"

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.200
Brent Spiner

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.140
Another legend of Trek, Brent Spiner's run
as Lieutenant Commander Data spanned fifteen

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.140
years.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.960
That includes seven seasons of The Next Generation
and four feature films, one of which earned

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.600
him a Saturn Award for best supporting actor.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.280
He also played Data's brother, Data's creator,
and even made an appearance in the prequel

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.849
series Enterprise, as Data's great-great-grandfather,
so his place in Trek spans more than just

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:13.870
one series.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.349
Spiner eventually put Trek behind him.

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:20.730
At one point, he even publicly took issue
with fans' perception of the show, saying

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.730
in 2011,

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.879
"We carry guns.

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:24.110
It's a joke.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.090
It's like that illusion that it is somehow
all about peace.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.840
It's really not."

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.240
"Oh, s---!"

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.450
After provoking the wrath of Trekkies everywhere,
Spiner's subsequent casting in Star Wars Rebels

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.829
must have struck a nerve.

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:43.660
The actor voiced Senator Gall Trayvis, a character
who, coincidentally, is a total traitor.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:44.560
No kidding, Brent.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.460
"No!"

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.810
​Ethan Phillips

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.239
Though he pops up in everything from Girls
to Veep to Homeboys in Outer Space, Ethan

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:02.560
Phillips' most memorable role remains Neelix,
the USS Voyager's chef and ambassador.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.099
Phillips portrayed the Talaxian for the show's
entire seven-season run, but he also made

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:10.549
three minor appearances as different Star
Trek characters.

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:15.459
In Next Generation and Enterprise, Phillips
played two different Ferengi, and he also

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:20.810
made a cameo appearance in Star Trek: First
Contact as a holographic maitre d'.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:25.150
Phillips has since become something of a regular
in the world of Star Wars video games, providing

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.559
the voice of transport pilot Hamman Flatt
in Force Commander.

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:33.340
He also played an Imperial medical droid,
Krantian governor and Royal grenade trooper

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.359
in 2002's Galactic Battlegrounds, as well
as several additional characters in 2003's

00:03:39.359 --> 00:03:40.650
Knights of the Old Republic.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.299
"Hello to you.

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:45.579
You’re new to The Academy, aren't you?"

00:03:45.579 --> 00:03:46.590
Simon Pegg

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.659
British actor Simon Pegg is known for many
roles, from his zombie-fighting hero in Shaun

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:55.730
of the Dead, to his recurring role in the
Mission Impossible film franchise.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:00.730
But Trek fans know him as Montgomery Scott
in J.J. Abrams' Star Trek reboot movies.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:05.379
He proved to be such as asset to Abrams that
the director later admitted he had an ulterior

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:09.540
motive when he cast him in the minor role
of Unkar Plutt in Star Wars: The Force Awakens,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.540
saying,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.199
"When we were shooting The Force Awakens in
Abu Dhabi he was there as an actor.

00:04:14.199 --> 00:04:19.320
But for me, he was there as a writer and film-maker,
and as someone to go around the issues I was

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.920
having at the time with the story and to get
some great feedback."

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.500
It probably didn't take much for Abrams to
convince Pegg to appear in the film, either:

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.730
"It's Star Wars.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.160
I'd burn for Star Wars."

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.130
Pegg's input didn't end there, either.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:38.870
He was also sought out by Paramount bosses
when Abrams left the Star Trek franchise,

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:44.100
bringing Pegg on board as a co-writer for
the third installment, Star Trek Beyond.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.370
Ron Perlman

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:49.950
Best known for playing the demonic anti-hero
Hellboy, as well as his starring role on Sons

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.070
of Anarchy, and a few hundred voice roles,
Ron Perlman has been around the block more

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:55.130
than once.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:59.771
Many of Perlman's hundreds of credits lie
in genre films, an area of cinema he's has

00:04:59.771 --> 00:05:00.860
always supported.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.130
So when the opportunity came along to join
the cast of 2002's Star Trek: Nemesis as the

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.440
Reman Viceroy, Perlman asked his manager for
the time and place.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.440
While he enjoyed working on the movie, neither
audiences nor critics seemed to share his

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.440
enthusiasm.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.130
Fortunately, his foray into Star Wars received
a much more positive reaction.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.370
Perlman voiced Gha Nachkt, a Trandoshan scavenger
who meets his untimely end at the hands of

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.610
General Grievous, in the highly acclaimed
first season of The Clone Wars.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.760
​Ed Begley Jr.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.260
Veteran actor and environmentalist Ed Begley
Jr. is probably best known for his seven-year

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:42.400
stint on St. Elsewhere, though he actually
has hundreds of TV and movie credits to his

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.400
name.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.841
Among them are his appearance on the Star
Trek: Voyager two-parter "Future's End", in

00:05:46.841 --> 00:05:51.660
which he took on the one-off role of 20th
century industrialist Henry Starling — who

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.280
just happens to be working with a character
played by Sarah Silverman.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.490
Begley Jr. has since said he "felt really
blessed" to be a part of the Star Trek franchise,

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.730
as a huge fan of the show, though that didn't
stop him from taking a gig with the rival

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.780
franchise the very same year.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.890
He was hired to provide the voice of Boba
Fett in the radio adaptation of Return of

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.230
the Jedi, produced with the blessing of George
Lucas.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.260
And speaking of Boba Fett…

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.260
Jason Wingreen

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:23.210
A victim of the dreaded Star Wars DVD editions,
Jason Wingreen saw his part as the voice of

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:28.160
Boba Fett in The Empire Strikes Back chopped
and replaced with the voice of Temuera Morrison,

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.960
the man who played Jango Fett in the prequel
trilogy.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.390
To add insult to injury, Wingreen was never
actually credited for his contribution to

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:39.770
the Star Wars universe, which only became
common knowledge four years before the great

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.380
remastering of '04.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:46.170
While he may be best remembered as Harry Snowden
from All in the Family, for Star Trek fans

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:51.150
he'll always be Doctor Linke, the Federation
scientist who gets sent to the uninhabited

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:56.780
planet Minara II to conduct studies in Original
Series episode "The Empath."

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:57.960
Deep Roy

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.800
The diminutive Deep Roy has an impressive
filmography stretching back about 40 years,

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.030
including two big screen Star Wars installments.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:11.410
Roy played musician Droopy McCool in Return
of the Jedi, a member of the Max Rebo Band,

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:15.500
the 12-piece ensemble that performs in Jabba
the Hutt's Tatooine palace.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.581
The four-foot-three actor actually appeared
uncredited in The Empire Strikes Back, as

00:07:19.581 --> 00:07:22.880
a body double for Yoda during a number of
scenes on Dagobah.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.020
Roy is also one of the very few actors to
appear on screen in both franchises, playing

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.920
Scotty's oyster-faced alien sidekick Keenser
in the rebooted Trek series.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:37.740
Keenser was never even meant to join Scotty
on the franchise's most famous starship but

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:42.270
Roy's performance was so memorable that they
invited him back to reprise his role.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.920
​Clancy Brown

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.639
Sci-fi fans shouldn't really need an introduction
to Clancy Brown.

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:51.170
He's played the immortal antagonist The Kurgan
in Highlander,

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:52.220
“Mom!

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:53.720
Woof!"

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.900
...and Sergeant Zim in Starship Troopers.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.889
Even your kids know Clancy Brown, even if
only in voice.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:02.680
"Donate to the children’s fund?

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:03.680
Why?

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:05.330
What have children ever done for me?”

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:11.270
Despite an impressive sci-fi resume, it wasn't
until 2002 that Brown showed up on Star Trek,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.020
taking on the role of Zobral in the Enterprise
episode "Desert Crossing."

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:19.610
In it, Archer and Tucker accept an invitation
to his homeworld — and unwittingly find

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.500
themselves caught up in a war.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:26.270
Among his countless voice roles, Brown also
dipped into Star Wars territory when he played

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:31.340
Darth Maul's brother Savage Opress in the
Clone Wars series, as well as Ryder Azadi

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.090
in Rebels… who only kind of looks exactly
like him.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:36.690
​Clive Revill

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:41.169
Renowned stage actor Clive Revill portrayed
a tweaked version of classic English character

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:47.640
Sir Guy of Gisbourne in 1991's Next Generation
episode "Qpid," which took the crew to the

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:48.800
12th-century world of Robin Hood.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.860
While it wasn't the most memorable performance
the New Zealand native has given over the

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:57.140
course of his distinguished career, at least
it wasn't replaced in later edits like his

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.700
work on the original Star Wars trilogy was.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:07.960
"Sorry.”

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:13.540
Revill supplied the voice of Emperor Palpatine
in the first cut of The Empire Strikes Back.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:19.240
But like so many others, his work was dubbed
over for the 2004 DVD release, replaced with

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.640
the voice of Ian McDiarmid, who took over
as the Emperor with Return of the Jedi.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:28.540
Revill was humble about the change, however,
conceding that continuity was important and

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:32.020
saying that the filmmakers "made a good choice
with McDiarmid."

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:37.180
Considering that the original 1980 emperor
was played by a woman with superimposed chimp

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:40.080
eyes, pretty much anything was an improvement.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.620
Greg Grunberg

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:46.150
As a self-proclaimed geek, Greg Grunberg couldn't
have asked for a better friend than the man

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:50.340
who rebooted Star Trek and kick-started the
third Star Wars trilogy.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:55.430
Grunberg has worked with J.J. Abrams almost
exclusively, so when Abrams began his cinematic

00:09:55.430 --> 00:10:00.050
reinvention of Star Trek, he called on Grunberg
again, drafting his friend to overdub the

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:02.060
dialogue for James Kirk's step-dad.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:03.700
"Hey, are you out of your mind?

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.470
That car’s an antique.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:08.112
You think you can get away with this just
because your mother’s off-planet?

00:10:08.112 --> 00:10:10.290
You get your ass back home, now!”

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:15.370
He appeared in person as Commander Finnegan
in 2016's Star Trek Beyond, though by that

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:18.060
point he had already crossed the franchise
divide.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:23.200
Grunberg played Resistance pilot Snap Wexley
in 2015's The Force Awakens, though he later

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.920
told StarTrek.com that he's on the fence about
which franchise is his favorite.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:28.920
Olivia D'Abo

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.700
"DABO!

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:32.620
Hahahaha!"

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:36.950
Best known to Trekkies as Starfleet Academy
intern Amanda Rogers from the Next Generation

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.520
episode "True Q," Olivia D'Abo —

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.590
“Dabo!”

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:41.650
Ahem.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:45.800
D'Abo is probably best known for playing Kevin
Arnold's hippie sister Karen in The Wonder

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:46.800
Years.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:47.870
Believe it or not, D'Abo —

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:50.040
“Dabo!”

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:54.710
— considers herself to be a pretty big sci
fi nerd, telling the official Star Trek website,

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:59.089
"I considered it a real honor to be working
on the continuation of the Star Trek legacy

00:10:59.089 --> 00:11:00.290
I'd grown up with.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:05.170
It had a huge effect on my childhood and not
unlike Star Wars it made me more curious about

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:09.870
space, other life forms in our galaxy and
the wonder of astronomy in general."

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:15.220
In 2008, she joined a number of her former
Star Trek colleagues at Lucasfilm to record

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:19.140
the voice of Luminara Unduli for The Clone
Wars animated series.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.320
And, oh yeah…

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.320
“Dabo!”

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:24.220
Patty Maloney

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:29.110
Emerging from the darkest day in Star Wars
history is Patty Maloney, who played a part

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.860
in the infamous and totally bizarre Star Wars
Holiday Special.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:36.970
The 3-foot-eleven actress was hired to play
the special's main character, Chewbacca's

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:39.960
son Lumpawaroo, better known as Lumpy.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.000
No, seriously.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:45.040
[Wookiee sounds]

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.870
Maloney's memories of the epically weird program
were completely positive, saying,

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.551
"We were all very pleased with the outcome
of it.

00:11:54.551 --> 00:11:55.551
Actually … I was.

00:11:55.551 --> 00:11:56.890
I was very pleased with it."

00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:02.110
The special aired in 1978 and, because of
the sheer shame involved, was never seen again

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.320
in any official capacity.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.410
Maloney's role in Star Trek was another one
created for a person of her stature.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:13.890
In the 1996 Voyager episode "The Thaw," she
plays one of the many creations of The Clown

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.339
— a manifestation of fear born of a linked
neural network.

00:12:17.339 --> 00:12:19.080
Because, you know, sci-fi.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.440
This time, she's credited as only "Little
Woman", which is still a huge improvement

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.960
over being a grunting idiot named "Lumpy".

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.529
Added bonus, it was even a speaking role!

00:12:28.529 --> 00:12:29.970
Oh, boy!

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:31.760
And she got to hang out with Michael McKean.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:33.080
Point, Star Trek.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.720
“A virus, a virus, he thinks I am a virus!”

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:40.920
Okay.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:42.600
We're taking that point back.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.600
Thanks for watching!

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.390
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.779
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

